WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.277
Logging is an operational safety feature.

2
00:00:02.277 --> 00:00:04.934
Developers use logs to understand unexpected behavior.

3
00:00:04.934 --> 00:00:07.971
Operators use them to monitor health and capacity.

4
00:00:07.971 --> 00:00:13.285
Responders use them to reconstruct timelines and confirm whether systems are behaving as intended.

5
00:00:13.285 --> 00:00:17.460
At the same time, logging is a security-sensitive design decision.

6
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:25.431
A log event can reveal secrets, collect unnecessary personal data, expose internal paths, or preserve implementation details long after the original

7
00:00:25.431 --> 00:00:26.569
request is gone.

8
00:00:26.569 --> 00:00:31.883
Logs can also become unreliable if they are noisy, inconsistent, or unavailable during failure.

9
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:36.438
Safe logging is not about logging as little as possible by default.

10
00:00:36.438 --> 00:00:38.336
It is about logging intentionally.

11
00:00:38.336 --> 00:00:45.927
Each event should have a purpose: debugging a class of failures, supporting operations, measuring reliability, meeting audit needs, or helping

12
00:00:45.927 --> 00:00:47.066
maintainers investigate responsibly.

13
00:00:47.066 --> 00:00:48.204
Controlled detail matters.

14
00:00:48.204 --> 00:00:54.277
A useful event identifies what happened, where it happened, and how it can be correlated safely.

15
00:00:54.277 --> 00:01:02.628
It should avoid dumping raw objects, large request bodies, or sensitive values simply because those values are available at the call site.

16
00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:07.182
C++ systems often run close to resources, concurrency, and lower-level libraries.

17
00:01:07.182 --> 00:01:12.496
Logging must therefore be reliable under pressure, safe during cleanup, and careful with formatting.

18
00:01:12.496 --> 00:01:16.292
A logging call should not destabilize an already failing path.

19
00:01:16.292 --> 00:01:18.569
Review logs as security-sensitive output.

20
00:01:18.569 --> 00:01:27.679
Ask whether the event helps a real operational need, whether fields are safe, whether access and retention are governed, and whether the message will

21
00:01:27.679 --> 00:01:30.336
remain understandable when systems are under stress.

22
00:01:30.336 --> 00:01:32.993
A mature logging strategy also defines ownership.

23
00:01:32.993 --> 00:01:42.102
Teams should know who approves new event fields, who responds to noisy alerts, and who decides when diagnostic detail should be reduced, masked, or

24
00:01:42.102 --> 00:01:44.000
retained for a shorter period.
